<DocsLayout title="Limitations" kicker="03 / adoption boundary" description="What reachq does not promise yet, and the questions to answer before using it as production infrastructure.">
  <h2>What is supported</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pure-Python directed-graph primitives and deterministic seeded constructions.</li>
    <li>Exact reachability-preservation checks for shortcut-set workflows.</li>
    <li>Weighted hopset construction with an explicit epsilon parameter and baseline comparison.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What is not promised</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No universal practical speedup follows from beta or hopbound alone.</li>
    <li>The hopset path is sequential in the current Python implementation.</li>
    <li>Research algorithms and optional acceleration backends can change without a stable API promise.</li>
    <li>There is no published wheel or hosted graph service yet.</li>
    <li>Exact transitive closure is output-quadratic and guarded by a pair budget.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="docs-callout"><p>Use NetworkX or igraph for general graph manipulation. Use reachq when its shortcut-set or hopset constructions are the specific thing you need.</p></div>
  <h2>Benchmark honestly</h2>
  <p>Record the commit, Python version, operating system, CPU, graph source, vertex and edge counts, seed, algorithm parameters, construction time, query time, memory, output size, and approximation error. A theoretical quantity is not a measured product speedup.</p>
</DocsLayout>
